<title>FRANCE: Kohl, Chirac rule out new budget pact talks.</title>
<headline>Kohl, Chirac rule out new budget pact talks.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1997-05-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>French President Jacques Chirac and German Chancellor Helmut Kohl on Tuesday ruled out any renegotiation of the European Union's &quot;stability pact&quot; meant to limit budget deficits under monetary union.</p>
<p>Kohl had been asked at a news conference following a meeting with Chirac what he thought of a demand by France's opposition Socialists for new talks on the stability pact and a European economic government.</p>
<p>&quot;No. I don't see why,&quot; Kohl said.</p>
<p>Chirac said that, &quot;France has given its commitment. Nobody would accept or understand if we were to go back on our commitments.&quot;</p>
<p>EU leaders agreed on the stability pact last December in Dublin after months of wrangling between France and Germany over what form it should take.</p>
<p>-- Paris Newsroom +33 1 4221 5452</p>
</text>
